================================================================================
Third-party components SHIPPED with the engine and Python bindings
================================================================================

CUDA Toolkit
  NVIDIA Corporation.
  Used for GPU runtime and compute (the production physics backend).
  Distributed and used under the NVIDIA CUDA Toolkit End User License Agreement
  (NVIDIA Software License Agreement). NVIDIA CUDA components are not relicensed
  by this project and remain subject to the NVIDIA SLA.

tinyxml2
  Copyright (c) Lee Thomason and the tinyxml2 contributors.
  Licensed under the zlib License.
  Used by the MJCF / URDF scene importers to parse XML. tinyxml2 (v10.0.0,
  fetched at build time) is compiled and STATICALLY linked into the engine
  library (libnuka), so it is present in the shipped Python runtime. (The
  `.usda` text-USD parser is separate and hand-written; see Notes below.)

Vulkan
  Used by the OPTIONAL offscreen renderer component (`nuka_render`), which links
  the Vulkan loader/headers and requires the Vulkan SDK (and its `glslc` shader
  compiler) to build the C++ engine. Vulkan is NOT linked into the shipped
  Python extension (`_nuka_ext`) runtime — that links only the engine library
  and the CUDA runtime (verified: libnuka's dynamic dependencies contain no
  Vulkan loader and no Vulkan symbols). Vulkan is a registered trademark of, and
  the Vulkan loader/headers are made available by, The Khronos Group Inc., under
  their respective licenses.

nanobind
  Copyright (c) Wenzel Jakob and the nanobind contributors.
  Licensed under the BSD 3-Clause License.
  Used to build the Python bindings (the `_nuka_ext` C++ extension module).

DLPack
  Copyright the DLPack contributors.
  Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
  Used for zero-copy tensor interop (PyTorch / JAX) via the DLPack protocol.

V-HACD (Volumetric Hierarchical Approximate Convex Decomposition)
  Copyright (c) 2011 Khaled Mamou and the V-HACD contributors.
  Licensed under the BSD 3-Clause License (see external/vhacd/LICENSE).
  Vendored as the single-header v4.1 release (external/vhacd/VHACD.h). Used at
  COOK TIME ONLY (offline / host CPU) by the asset cooker to decompose concave
  collision meshes into convex pieces; it is a one-time pre-simulation step and
  is NOT part of the GPU runtime / per-step physics path (master plan §5.6
  sanctioned exception). The implementation is compiled into a single
  translation unit (src/import/cooker/convex_decomposition.cpp).

SHA-256 (content-hash cache key)
  Derived from the public-domain SHA-256 reference implementation by Brad Conte
  ("crypto-algorithms"), released into the public domain (see
  external/sha256/LICENSE). Reformatted into a single namespaced C++ header
  (external/sha256/sha256.h). Used at COOK TIME ONLY to content-hash the convex
  decomposition inputs (mesh bytes + serialized params) for the dedup cache.

Philox4x32-10 counter-based RNG (algorithm)
  The Philox4x32-10 counter-based pseudo-random number generator used in this
  project is SELF-IMPLEMENTED; no third-party Philox/Random123 source code is
  vendored. The algorithm is cited for attribution:
    John K. Salmon, Mark A. Moraes, Ron O. Dror, and David E. Shaw,
    "Parallel Random Numbers: As Easy as 1, 2, 3,"
    Proceedings of the International Conference for High Performance Computing,
    Networking, Storage and Analysis (SC '11), ACM, 2011.

================================================================================
Components used at BUILD or TEST time ONLY (NOT shipped in the runtime binary)
================================================================================

PyTorch / libtorch
  Copyright The PyTorch / Linux Foundation contributors.
  libtorch is NOT linked into the Python extension and is NOT used to build it:
  the `_nuka_ext` extension links only against libnuka and the CUDA runtime
  (cudart) and pins the C++11 ABI explicitly (`_GLIBCXX_USE_CXX11_ABI=1`) rather
  than detecting it from Torch (verified by inspecting the extension's dynamic
  dependencies). libtorch IS required by, and linked into, the OPTIONAL C++
  inference tool under `tools/inference/` (CMake `find_package(Torch)`), which is
  not part of the Python package. PyTorch is additionally supported as an
  OPTIONAL runtime interop target (zero-copy DLPack tensor exchange). In none of
  these roles is PyTorch a required dependency of the shipped Python runtime.

JAX
  Copyright The JAX Authors / Google LLC.
  Optional RUNTIME interop target only (zero-copy DLPack tensor exchange).
  Not a required dependency and not vendored.

Eigen
  Copyright the Eigen contributors.
  Licensed under the Mozilla Public License, Version 2.0 (MPL-2.0).
  TEST-ONLY: used solely as a dense linear-algebra oracle in the test suite for
  differential validation. The production sparse solver is self-written; Eigen
  is never present in any production / shipped code path.

GoogleTest
  Copyright Google LLC.
  Licensed under the BSD 3-Clause License.
  TEST-ONLY: the C++ unit/regression test framework.

scikit-build-core
  Copyright the scikit-build contributors.
  Build backend only (used to build the Python package / extension); not shipped
  as a runtime dependency.

nanobind (build backend)
  In addition to being a shipped binding library (above), nanobind's CMake
  integration is used as part of the Python build backend.

================================================================================
Notes
================================================================================

The `.usda` (text USD) scene parser in this project is hand-written using only
the C++ standard library. There is NO OpenUSD / tinyusdz dependency, and no USD
SDK is linked into or shipped with the runtime; therefore no USD attribution is
included here.
